.TH EJSON 5 "January 2026" "ejson" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
ejson \- encrypted secrets file format for JSON, TOML, and YAML
.SH SYNOPSIS
An
.B ejson
file is a JSON, TOML, or YAML file with encrypted string values using
public-key cryptography. This page describes the file format and encryption
rules common to all supported formats.
.PP
By convention, files are saved with extensions that indicate their format:
.IR .ejson " or " .json
for JSON,
.IR .etoml " or " .toml
for TOML, and
.IR .eyaml ", " .eyml ", " .yaml ", or " .yml
for YAML.
.SH PUBLIC KEY
Each secrets file must have a top-level key named
.BR _public_key .
This implies that the top-level structure must be an object (or table/mapping),
not an array or list.
.PP
The
.B _public_key
value must be a string containing a public key as generated by
.BR ejson\-keygen (1).
For the legacy
.B v1
scheme this is a hex-encoded 32-byte (64 ASCII characters) key. For the hybrid
post-quantum
.B v2
scheme it begins with
.I v2:
followed by a base64-encoded X25519 public key concatenated with an ML-KEM-768
encapsulation key.
.PP
By convention,
.B _public_key
should be the first key in the file.
.PP
.B JSON example:
.RS
.nf
{
  "_public_key": "63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f",
  "database_password": "supersecret"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B TOML example:
.RS
.nf
_public_key = "63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f"
database_password = "supersecret"
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B YAML example:
.RS
.nf
_public_key: "63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f"
database_password: "supersecret"
.fi
.RE
.SH ENCRYPTABLE VALUES
A value is considered encryptable if:
.IP 1. 4
It is a string literal. Numbers, booleans (true/false), null/nil, and dates
remain unencrypted.
.IP 2. 4
It is not an object key (i.e., not a key name, only values are encrypted).
.IP 3. 4
Its corresponding key does not begin with an underscore
.RB ( _ ).
.PP
Take special note of rule 3. This is why
.B _public_key
is not encrypted, and can be used to construct metadata schemes. For example,
in the excerpt below, only the values for
.I rotation_password
and
.I secret
will be encrypted:
.PP
.RS
.nf
"my_secret": {
  "_description": "API key for foocorp",
  "_rotation": "https://example.com/foocorp/apikey",
  "_rotation_username": "admin",
  "rotation_password": "password",
  "secret": "123123123123123123123"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Also note that the underscore "unencryptable" attribute does
.B not
inherit to nested values. For example, the password in this excerpt
.I will
be encrypted:
.PP
.RS
.nf
"_unencryptable": {
  "password": "encrypted anyway"
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Arrays
are processed element-by-element. Each string element in an array is
encrypted individually, while non-string elements remain unchanged.
.SH ENCRYPTED VALUE FORMAT
Encrypted values are schema-tagged. The legacy
.B v1
form is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
EJ[1:P:N:M]
.fi
.RE
.PP
where the fields are:
.TP
.B P
(base64-encoded 32-byte array) Public key of an ephemeral keypair used to
encrypt this value. A new ephemeral keypair is generated for each encryption
session, providing forward secrecy.
.TP
.B N
(base64-encoded 24-byte array) Nonce used to encrypt this value.
.TP
.B M
(base64-encoded variable-length array) Raw ciphertext (encrypted data with
Poly1305 authentication tag).
.PP
The hybrid post-quantum
.B v2
form is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
EJ[2:E:C:N:M]
.fi
.RE
.PP
where the fields are:
.TP
.B E
(base64-encoded 32-byte array) Ephemeral X25519 public key, fresh per value.
.TP
.B C
(base64-encoded 1088-byte array) ML-KEM-768 ciphertext (encapsulated shared
secret), fresh per value.
.TP
.B N
(base64-encoded 24-byte array) XChaCha20-Poly1305 nonce.
.TP
.B M
(base64-encoded variable-length array) Raw ciphertext with Poly1305
authentication tag.
.PP
Existing
.B v1
values remain decryptable; a document's scheme is determined by its
.B _public_key.
.PP
.B Example encrypted values:
.RS
.nf
EJ[1:WGj2t4znULHT1IRveMEdvvNXqZzNBNMsJ5iZVy6Dvxs=:kA6ekF8ViYR5ZLeSmMXWsdLfWr7wn9qS:fcHQtdt6nqcNOXa97/M278RX6w==]
EJ[2:Yn1E5ivrva7dRnKi0l3uNhx2ASK0ZFwksCbc+5BnjAA=:WPeF38A/...(1088 bytes)...:hW4QJGhs3inwgJclTboHOFn+8xtYP0gv:SOWXjCLtw9j6Yte5n6X2iII=]
.fi
.RE
.SH CRYPTOGRAPHY
The legacy
.B v1
scheme uses NaCl Box for authenticated public-key encryption:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Curve25519
\- Elliptic curve Diffie-Hellman key agreement
.IP \(bu 2
.B XSalsa20
\- Stream cipher for encryption
.IP \(bu 2
.B Poly1305
\- Message authentication code
.PP
The hybrid
.B v2
scheme combines X25519 and ML-KEM-768 (FIPS 203) via HKDF-SHA256, then encrypts
with XChaCha20-Poly1305, so that an attacker must break both the classical and the
post-quantum primitive.
.PP
Both schemes provide:
.IP \(bu 2
.B Confidentiality
\- Only holders of the private key can decrypt values
.IP \(bu 2
.B Integrity
\- Any tampering with encrypted values is detected
.IP \(bu 2
.B Forward secrecy
\- Each encryption uses fresh ephemeral key material
.SH FORMAT-SPECIFIC NOTES
.SS JSON
.IP \(bu 2
Standard JSON syntax with string, number, boolean, null, object, and array types
.IP \(bu 2
Comments are not supported
.IP \(bu 2
File extensions:
.IR .ejson ", " .json
.SS TOML
.IP \(bu 2
Supports TOML 1.0 syntax including tables, inline tables, and arrays
.IP \(bu 2
Date/time values remain unencrypted
.IP \(bu 2
Comments (lines starting with #) are preserved
.IP \(bu 2
File extensions:
.IR .etoml ", " .toml
.SS YAML
.IP \(bu 2
Supports YAML 1.2 syntax including mappings, sequences, and anchors/aliases
.IP \(bu 2
Comments (lines starting with #) are preserved
.IP \(bu 2
Multi-line strings (literal | and folded >) are supported
.IP \(bu 2
File extensions:
.IR .eyaml ", " .eyml ", " .yaml ", " .yml
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.B Complete JSON file (before encryption):
.RS
.nf
{
  "_public_key": "63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f",
  "_comment": "Production database credentials",
  "database": {
    "_host": "db.example.com",
    "username": "admin",
    "password": "supersecret123"
  },
  "api_keys": ["key1", "key2", "key3"]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Complete JSON file (after encryption):
.RS
.nf
{
  "_public_key": "63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f",
  "_comment": "Production database credentials",
  "database": {
    "_host": "db.example.com",
    "username": "EJ[1:abc...]:...:...]",
    "password": "EJ[1:def...]:...:...]"
  },
  "api_keys": ["EJ[1:...]", "EJ[1:...]", "EJ[1:...]"]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Complete TOML file (before encryption):
.RS
.nf
_public_key = "63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f"

# Production database credentials
_comment = "Do not share these credentials"

[database]
_host = "db.example.com"
username = "admin"
password = "supersecret123"
port = 5432  # Numbers are not encrypted
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B Complete YAML file (before encryption):
.RS
.nf
_public_key: "63ccf05a9492e68e12eeb1c705888aebdcc0080af7e594fc402beb24cce9d14f"

# Production database credentials
_comment: "Do not share these credentials"

database:
  _host: db.example.com
  username: admin
  password: supersecret123
  port: 5432  # Numbers are not encrypted

allowed_hosts:
  - host1.example.com
  - host2.example.com
.fi
.RE
